.TH FILE 3 "2026" "y-- standard library" "y-- Value Types"
.SH NAME
file \- built-in file value type methods
.SH DESCRIPTION
File objects represent open file handles for reading and writing data on the filesystem.
They are typically instantiated using functions from the \fBFileStream\fR module.
.SH METHODS
.TP
\fBIsOpen()\fR -> bool()
Returns true if the file is currently open and accessible. Calling most other methods on a closed file will throw a FileClosedError.
.TP
\fBWrite(message, [replace=false])\fR -> none()
Writes the string representation of \fBmessage\fR to the file. If \fBreplace\fR is false (default), the text is appended to the end. If true, the file is overwritten from the beginning.
.TP
\fBClear()\fR -> none()
Resets and clears the entire contents of the file, making it empty.
.TP
\fBRead()\fR -> string()
Reads the entire contents of the file into a single string and returns it.
.TP
\fBReadLines([include_newline=false])\fR -> list()
Reads the file line by line, returning a list of strings. By default, trailing newline characters are stripped from each line unless \fBinclude_newline\fR is true.
.TP
\fBClose()\fR -> none()
Closes the file handle, flushing any pending writes and releasing operating system resources.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
FileStream(3)
